issue_body:
Calling `.int()` after explicit numeric constraints changes the generated JSON Schema, even though runtime validation behaves identically.

Tested with:

- Zod 4.5.4
- Node.js 24.18.0
- JSON Schema Draft 2020-12 and OpenAPI 3.0 targets

### Minimal reproduction

```ts
import * as z from "zod";

const intFirst = z.number().int().min(0).max(23);
const intLast = z.number().min(0).max(23).int();

for (const target of ["draft-2020-12", "openapi-3.0"] as const) {
  console.log(target);
  console.log("intFirst", z.toJSONSchema(intFirst, { target }));
  console.log("intLast", z.toJSONSchema(intLast, { target }));
}
```

### Actual output

For both targets:

```ts
// z.number().int().min(0).max(23)
{
  type: "integer",
  minimum: 0,
  maximum: 23
}

// z.number().min(0).max(23).int()
{
  type: "integer",
  minimum: -9007199254740991,
  maximum: 9007199254740991
}
```

Both schemas still behave identically during runtime validation:

- `-1` is rejected
- `0` is accepted
- `1.5` is rejected
- `23` is accepted
- `24` is rejected

### Expected output

The explicit `minimum: 0` and `maximum: 23` constraints should be preserved regardless of whether `.int()` appears before or after them.

At minimum, the generated schema should not describe different accepted ranges when the two Zod schemas accept the same values.

This matters when `toJSONSchema()` is used to produce an OpenAPI contract: applying `.int()` last causes the published contract to lose the application's explicit range.

### Related issue

[#4779](https://github.com/colinhacks/zod/issues/4779) discusses the default safe-integer range emitted for an unconstrained integer. This case is different because explicit, tighter constraints are present and enforced at runtime, but are replaced only in the generated schema when `.int()` is applied last.

`z.toZod<T>()` accepts schemas when a property is typed as a union of enum members:
`someEnum: SomeEnum.A | SomeEnum.B;`
However, when the property is typed as the enum itself:
`someEnum: SomeEnum;`
The corresponding Zod schemas are rejected by TypeScript, even though they represent the same set of enum values.

### Environment

- `typescript@6.0.3`
- `zod@4.5.4`

### Reproduction

When the interface property is a **union of enum members**, the type check passes:

```ts
enum SomeEnum {
  A = "A",
  B = "B",
}

interface SomeObject {
  someEnum: SomeEnum.A | SomeEnum.B;
}

const schema = z.toZod<SomeObject>()(
  z.object({
    someEnum: z.enum(SomeEnum),
    // also works:
    // someEnum: z.enum([SomeEnum.A, SomeEnum.B]),
    // someEnum: z.union([z.literal(SomeEnum.A), z.literal(SomeEnum.B)]),
  }),
);
```

However, when the interface property is typed as the **enum itself**, the same schemas are rejected:

```ts
enum SomeEnum {
  A = "A",
  B = "B",
}

interface SomeObject {
  someEnum: SomeEnum;
}

const schema = z.toZod<SomeObject>()(
  z.object({
    someEnum: z.enum(SomeEnum),
    // same failure with:
    // someEnum: z.enum([SomeEnum.A, SomeEnum.B]),
    // someEnum: z.union([z.literal(SomeEnum.A), z.literal(SomeEnum.B)]),
  }),
);
```

### TypeScript error

```txt
Argument of type 'ZodObject<{ someEnum: ZodUnion<readonly [ZodLiteral<SomeEnum.A>, ZodLiteral<SomeEnum.B>]>; }, $strip>' is not assignable to parameter of type '$ZodObject<{ someEnum: ToZodKeyMismatch<SomeEnum, SomeEnum>; }, $strip>'.
  Types of property '_zod' are incompatible.
    Type '$ZodObjectInternals<{ someEnum: ZodUnion<readonly [ZodLiteral<SomeEnum.A>, ZodLiteral<SomeEnum.B>]>; }, $strip>' is not assignable to type '$ZodObjectInternals<{ someEnum: ToZodKeyMismatch<SomeEnum, SomeEnum>; }, $strip>'.
      Type '{ someEnum: ZodUnion<readonly [ZodLiteral<SomeEnum.A>, ZodLiteral<SomeEnum.B>]>; }' is not assignable to type '{ someEnum: ToZodKeyMismatch<SomeEnum, SomeEnum>; }'.
        Types of property 'someEnum' are incompatible.
          Type 'ZodUnion<readonly [ZodLiteral<SomeEnum.A>, ZodLiteral<SomeEnum.B>]>' is not assignable to type 'ToZodKeyMismatch<SomeEnum, SomeEnum>'.
            Property '"types do not match"' is missing in type 'ZodUnion<readonly [ZodLiteral<SomeEnum.A>, ZodLiteral<SomeEnum.B>]>' but required in type '{ "types do not match": { expected: SomeEnum; received: SomeEnum; }; }'.ts(2345)
util.d.cts(25, 5): '"types do not match"' is declared here.
```

### Expected behavior

`toZod<SomeObject>()` should accept a schema when its inferred output type is compatible with the corresponding property type.

In particular, a schema representing all members of `SomeEnum` should be accepted for a property typed as `SomeEnum`, just as the equivalent union of enum members is accepted.

### Actual behavior

The same set of enum values is accepted when the property is typed as `SomeEnum.A | SomeEnum.B`, but rejected when the property is typed as `SomeEnum`.

### Notes

This appears to be a type-level compatibility issue in `toZod`'s handling of enum types, rather than a runtime validation issue.

Related discussion: https://github.com/colinhacks/zod/pull/5913#issuecomment-5370100533

This code with Zod 4.5.2:

```
import * as z from "zod";

const schema = z.record(z.number(), z.number());
const jsonSchema = z.toJSONSchema(schema);
console.log(jsonSchema);
```

produces this output:

```
{
  '$schema': 'https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema',
  type: 'object',
  propertyNames: { type: 'number' },
  additionalProperties: { type: 'number' }
}
```

which can never work because in JSON the property names are always strings.

Similar issue in TypeScript with `Record<number, number>` being kind of weird, but Zod already handles that correctly (as [the docs](https://zod.dev/api?id=zrecord#zrecord) say, "Zod supports numeric keys inside records in a way that closely mirrors TypeScript itself"). So for `toJSONSchema` wouldn't it make sense to also support numeric keys in a sensible way?

The output might be better as:

```
{
  '$schema': 'https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema',
  type: 'object',
  propertyNames: { type: 'string' },
  additionalProperties: { type: 'number' }
}
```

But that's redundant since property names are always strings, so:

```
{
  '$schema': 'https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema',
  type: 'object',
  additionalProperties: { type: 'number' }
}
```

(You could imagine  doing something like `"propertyNames": { "pattern": "^[0-9]+$" }` but idk if that's worth the trouble because it'd have to be a fairly complex regex to handle negative numbers, decimals, NaN, Infinity, maybe more?)

If you define an object type and use a function for a property's default value, the function is executed twice:
```js
let test = 0;
const schema = z.object({

    TEST: z.string().default(() => {
        console.log(`Executed... ${test++}`);
        return 'default';
    })

})

schema.parse({});
// Executed... 0
// Executed... 1
```

When it's not wrapped in an object, the function is only executed once, as expected:
```js
const defaultTuna = z.string().default(() => {
    console.log(`tuna`);
    return 'tuna';
});

defaultTuna.parse(undefined); // => "tuna"
```

I'm currently using Node.js v24.14.1 and Zod v4.5.2. 
I have not checked the versions in between, but Zod v4.4.2 does not have this issue and the default function is only executed once.

**Repo:** `opentabs-dev/opentabs`
**Affected package(s):** `@opentabs-dev/opentabs-plugin-outlook` (v0.0.95) — and any plugin using `z.iso.datetime()` in a tool input schema
**Root cause:** zod v4.4.3 `z.iso.datetime()` regex builder
**Severity:** High — breaks `get_calendar_view`, `get_schedule`, `create_event`, `update_event` for MCP clients with strict schema compilers

---

## Summary

Tools that declare `start`/`end` params with `z.iso.datetime({ offset, local })` produce a JSON Schema whose `pattern` contains an **empty alternation branch** (`||`). Several strict JSON Schema compilers reject empty alternations, so the tool schema fails to compile and the tool cannot be called. The error surfaces as:

```
Tool "outlook__get_calendar_view" cannot be called: schema compilation failed. Schema compilation failed — validation skipped
```

## Root cause

In zod v4.4.3, `zod/v4/core/regexes.js` `datetime()`:

```js
export function datetime(args) {
    const time = timeSource({ precision: args.precision });
    const opts = ["Z"];
    if (args.local)
        opts.push("");                       // <-- empty string pushed
    if (args.offset)
        opts.push(`([+-](?:[01]\\d|2[0-3]):[0-5]\\d)`);
    const timeRegex = `${time}(?:${opts.join("|")})`;   // <-- join produces "||"
    return new RegExp(`^${dateSource}T(?:${timeRegex})$`);
}
```

When `local: true` is set, an empty string `""` is pushed into `opts`. When `offset` is also enabled, `opts` becomes `["Z", "", "([+-]...")]` and `join("|")` yields:

```
(?:Z||([+-](?:[01]\d|2[0-3]):[0-5]\d))
```

The `||` is an empty alternation branch. Even with only `local: true` (no offset), the pattern is `(?:Z|)` — a trailing empty branch — which some strict compilers also reject.

## Affected tools (in the Outlook plugin)

| Tool | Params | Generated pattern |
|------|--------|-------------------|
| `get_calendar_view` | `{ offset: true, local: true }` | `(?:Z\|\|([+-]...))` ❌ |
| `get_schedule` | `{ offset: false, local: true }` | `(?:Z\|)` ❌ |
| `create_event` | `{ offset: false, local: true }` | `(?:Z\|)` ❌ |
| `update_event` | `{ offset: false, local: true }` | `(?:Z\|)` ❌ |

## Reproduction

1. Load the Outlook plugin (v0.0.95) in an MCP client whose schema compiler rejects empty alternations.
2. Call `get_calendar_view` with any `start`/`end`.
3. Observe: `schema compilation failed`.

## Suggested fix

In zod's `datetime()` builder, avoid pushing an empty string into the alternation. Build the suffix as a single optional group instead:

```js
export function datetime(args) {
    const time = timeSource({ precision: args.precision });
    const suffix = [];
    if (args.offset) suffix.push(`[+-](?:[01]\\d|2[0-3]):[0-5]\\d`);
    const tz = args.offset ? `(?:Z|${suffix[0]})?` : args.local ? `Z?` : `Z`;
    const timeRegex = `${time}${tz}`;
    return new RegExp(`^${dateSource}T(?:${timeRegex})$`);
}
```

Or, minimally, only push `""` when `local` is true **and** `offset` is false, and guard the join so no empty branch is emitted.

For some reason the code below fails in a really weird way. With the given input both `gentleXor` and `strictXor` must successfully parse the input but `gentleXor` fails.

```typescript
import z from "zod"

const input = {
    "os": {
        "name": "windows",
        "version": "^10\\."
    }
}

const nameOsRule = z.object({
    name: z.enum(["windows", "osx", "linux"]).nonoptional()
})

const versionOsRule = nameOsRule.extend({
    version: z.string().nonempty().nonoptional()
})

// This will break
const gentleXor = z.xor([
    nameOsRule,
    versionOsRule
])

// The strict version works fine
const strictXor = z.xor([
    z.strictObject(nameOsRule.shape),
    z.strictObject(versionOsRule.shape)
])

gentleXor.parse(input.os, { reportInput: true })
strictXor.parse(input.os, { reportInput: true })
```

The error doesn't give any clue and looks uninformative:
```
ZodError: [
  {
    "code": "invalid_union",
    "errors": [],
    "inclusive": false,
    "path": [],
    "message": "Invalid input",
    "input": {
      "name": "windows",
      "version": "^10\\."
    }
  }
]
```

Can someone explain me why this breaks?

Zod version is 4.4.3

**Not a vulnerability report** — no prototype pollution occurs; the existing defense works. This is a question about `.strict()`'s reporting semantics that falls out of where that defense sits.

### What happens

`z.object(...).strict()` (and `z.strictObject`) reports every unknown input key as `unrecognized_keys` — except an own `__proto__` key, which is silently accepted and silently dropped from the parse output. So strict mode flags every unknown key except the one an attacker would actually send, and downstream code that inspects `parsed.data` never learns the key was there.

### Repro (zod 4.4.3, fresh install, Node 24)

```js
import { z } from 'zod';

const strict = z.object({ name: z.string() }).strict();

// JSON.parse creates an OWN "__proto__" property — the wire reality for any JSON body.
const wire = JSON.parse('{"name":"a","__proto__":{"admin":true}}');
console.log(Object.getOwnPropertyNames(wire)); // [ 'name', '__proto__' ]

const res = strict.safeParse(wire);
console.log(res.success);                                          // true   (expected: false)
console.log(Object.getOwnPropertyNames(res.data));                 // [ 'name' ]  — dropped
console.log(Object.getPrototypeOf(res.data) === Object.prototype); // true — defense intact 👍

// Control: any other unknown key is reported as documented.
console.log(strict.safeParse(JSON.parse('{"name":"a","other":1}')).success); // false, unrecognized_keys
```

### Where it comes from

The pollution guard in `handleCatchall` skips `__proto__` **before** the `unrecognized.push(key)` branch:

https://github.com/colinhacks/zod/blob/912f0f51b0ced654d0069741e7160834dca742ee/packages/zod/src/v4/core/schemas.ts#L1870-L1878

```ts
for (const key in input) {
  // skip __proto__ so it can't replace the result prototype via the
  // assignment setter on the plain {} we build into
  if (key === "__proto__") continue;   // ← also exempts it from the strict check below
  if (keySet.has(key)) continue;
  if (t === "never") {
    unrecognized.push(key);
    continue;
  }
```

The comment justifies not *copying* the key into the result (clearly right, please keep it). It doesn't say the key should also be exempt from strict's *reporting* — that looks like a side effect of where the `continue` sits. `z.record` has the same silent skip (schemas.ts#L2957 (same permalink base)).

### Why it matters

For anyone using `.strict()` as a trust-boundary contract ("a body carrying anything unexpected is refused loudly, never stripped silently"), this is the one input where the contract silently doesn't hold — and because the key is also absent from the output, a downstream check can't catch it either. We hit this in an e-signature API where a rejected-extra-keys guarantee is load-bearing; our unit tests proved the downstream authority rejects a smuggled `__proto__`, while the deployed pipeline never showed it that key at all. (Worked around with an own-key check beside the parse.)

### Suggested fix, if unintended

Report before skipping — keeps the copy-defense byte-for-byte identical:

```ts
if (key === "__proto__") {
  if (t === "never") unrecognized.push(key);
  continue;
}
```

Happy to send a PR with tests if you'd take this. And if the current behavior is intentional, a line in the `.strict()` docs naming the exemption would let integrators know to pin own-keys themselves.

### Description

For a `z.tuple([...])` with **no** `.rest()`, `.parse()` / `.safeParse()` strictly rejects arrays that are longer or shorter than the tuple. `.toJSONSchema()` does not reproduce this constraint faithfully in **any** target:

| target | positional item validation | length bound (`maxItems`/closed) |
|---|---|---|
| `draft-7` | ✅ correct | ❌ missing — extra trailing items pass unchecked |
| `draft-2020-12` | ✅ correct | ❌ missing (`items: false` never emitted) |
| `openapi-3.0` | ❌ **lost** — collapses to `items: { anyOf: [...] }`, so element *order* is no longer enforced | ✅ `maxItems`/`minItems` present |

No target matches `.parse()`'s accept set: `draft-7`/`draft-2020-12` leave the tail unbounded; `openapi-3.0` bounds the length but loses per-index typing (verified with reordered/duplicate elements).

This matters for consumers like ajv (e.g. via Fastify) that validate only against the emitted schema, not zod's runtime.

### Reproduction

```ts
import { z } from "zod";

const schema = z.tuple([
  z.string(),
  z.string(),
  z.object({ lang: z.string() }),
]);

schema.safeParse(["a", "b", { lang: "en" }, "extra"]).success;
// -> false (runtime: "too_big", maximum: 3)

console.log(JSON.stringify(schema.toJSONSchema({ target: "draft-7" })));
// {"type":"array","items":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"string"},
//  {"type":"object","properties":{"lang":{"type":"string"}},"required":["lang"],"additionalProperties":false}]}
// -> no "maxItems", no "additionalItems": false

console.log(JSON.stringify(schema.toJSONSchema({ target: "openapi-3.0" })));
// {"type":"array","items":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"string"},
//  {"type":"object","properties":{"lang":{"type":"string"}},"required":["lang"],"additionalProperties":false}]},
//  "minItems":3,"maxItems":3}
// -> length is bounded, but positional typing is gone:
//    ["a","a","a"] now validates, even though schema.safeParse(["a","a","a"]) rejects it
//    (index 2 must be the `{lang}` object, not a string)
```

Feeding the `draft-7` schema to ajv, `["a","b",{lang:"en"},null]` (unvalidated 4th element) passes. Feeding the `openapi-3.0` schema to ajv, `["a","a","a"]` (no `{lang}` object anywhere) also passes. Both diverge from `schema.safeParse()`.

### Root cause (from reading `v4/core/*`)

- `schemas.ts` (`$ZodTuple.init`): when `def.rest == null`, the parser enforces `input.length > items.length` directly … independent of `schema._zod.bag`.
- `json-schema-processors.ts` (`tupleProcessor`):
  - `draft-7`/`draft-2020-12`: only closes the tail (`additionalItems: false` / `items: false`) when `def.rest` is set; otherwise nothing marks the schema as closed.
  - `openapi-3.0`: correctly infers `maxItems`/`minItems` from `prefixItems.length` when `!rest`, but represents `items` as `{ anyOf: prefixItems }`, which is positionally unsound (OpenAPI 3.0's `items` only accepts a single schema, so this is the best available approximation, but it silently discards per-index typing).

The two families of targets fail in complementary but both incorrect ways; there's no existing target that gets both right.

### Expected behavior

For a `.rest()`-less tuple:
- `draft-7`: `additionalItems: false`
- `draft-2020-12`: `items: false`
- `openapi-3.0`: ideally reject/warn that a closed tuple can't be represented losslessly in OpenAPI 3.0 `items`, rather than silently producing a schema with different accept semantics (or emit `minItems`/`maxItems` *and* document that positional constraints are unenforceable in that target).

### Environment

- zod: 4.4.3
- Node.js: v24.18.0

When defining a Record with enums as keys and values with defaults, then zod is able to complete the missing keys with the default values in the parsed result:
```ts
import z from "zod"

const schema = z.record(z.enum(["UP", "DOWN"]), z.string().default("unknown"))

type Input = z.input<typeof schema>

// @ts-ignore
const input: Input = {
    UP: "somewhere"
}

console.log(z.parse(schema, input))
```
output:
```
{ UP: 'somewhere', DOWN: 'unknown' }
```

The only issue is that the inferred type `Input` is:
```ts
type Input = {
    UP: string | undefined;
    DOWN: string | undefined;
}
```
which requires using `// @ts-ignore` when defining partial inputs, otherwise typescript complains with:
```
error TS2741: Property 'DOWN' is missing in type '{ UP: string; }' but required in type 'Record<"UP" | "DOWN", string | undefined>
```

Assuming it is intended for zod to generate the missing fields in the output, then it would be amazing if the inferred `z.input` type to be Partial. For example:
```ts
type Input = Partial<{
    UP: string | undefined;
    DOWN: string | undefined;
}>
```

In zod3, I was able to specify a global error message

`z.number({errorMap: () => ({ message: 'my error' })}).min(1).max(99, 'my special message')`

When safeParse gets presented the following data:
- undefined
-  0
- 100

**zod 3** would always respond with the following errors:
- --> my error
- --> my error
- --> my special message

which made perfectly sense imho. 

**zod 4**, with the exact same schema - would always respond with: 
- --> Invalid input: expected number, received undefined
- --> Too small: expected string to have >=1 characters
- --> my  special message

How can I get the old behavior back? I **never ever** want any default zod error messages. Neither z.setErrorMap nor z.config are an option because I want *schema specific* error messages. I really need a fallback if no specific error message is defined. It's overly verbose to repeat any error message for every schema-rule. Additionally I now can't even pass undefined and there seems to be no way at all to override the error returned then.

I need context specific user friendly error messages.

### What I tried so far
I tried to postprocess the error messages by adding an own meta-information containing my error message. I could then return this error message in case of an parse-error. But I fail to detect if the error message from zod is either a custom one or the default one. If it's the default --> return my own "global" message, if its a custom one --> return the custom one.

```
