    }
```

Zod 4 function schemas are close to ideal for introspectable function definitions:

```ts
export const install = z.function()
  .input(z.tuple([z.string().describe('package name')]))
  .implement(pkg => ({installed: pkg, success: true}))
```

But not ideal, because we can't get the `input` schema back out of the above anywhere.

Use case: [`trpc-cli`](https://github.com/mmkal/trpc-cli) can turn a plain module of exported functions into CLI commands. Right now it only supports a vendored/patched `typebox` which can read plain-typescript types (so `export function install(name: string) {return {...}}` works roughly like the above.

It'd be great for advanced use cases to use `z.function` like the above, since parsing typescript types doesn't allow for runtime transforms/custom metadata etc. It can't work now because the input schema is lost.

I read you saying before that most proposed uses around `z.function()` as a schema are anti-patterns. I think this one is narrower:

- no additional validation steps
- not asking to restore v3 `z.function()` semantics
- just asking to expose an already-tracked-internally schema as a gettable property

## Current behavior

`ZodFunction` has schema:

```ts
const Fn = z.function().input(...)
Fn._def.input // usable at runtime, albeit internal

const fn = Fn.implement(pkg => ({installed: pkg, success: true}))

fn._def // undefined
fn._zod // undefined
Object.getOwnPropertySymbols(fn) // []
```

It could be non-enumerable, maybe symbol-keyed, read-only. Could return `undefined` for non-Zod fns.

Typing could be:

```ts
type ImplementedZodFunction<T extends z.ZodFunction> = T['_input'] & {
  _zod: T
}
```

## Related

- #4143: Zod 4 `z.function()` replacement / anti-pattern discussion
- #4842: extracting fn signature from Zod function schema
- #5121: `z.function()` extends `$ZodType`
- #313: older request for lighter function syntax; Zod 4 mostly solved syntax side

## Implementation

Hopefully `issue` is more valuable than `pull request` these days, but I did an implementation just to double-check that it is indeed easy and confirm that the maintenance burden would be near zero. Here's the full implementation diff. I'd be happy to open a PR and add tests + brief docs if ur ok with this:

```diff
diff --git a/packages/zod/src/v4/core/schemas.ts b/packages/zod/src/v4/core/schemas.ts
index 4f85fbf0..2c414284 100644
--- a/packages/zod/src/v4/core/schemas.ts
+++ b/packages/zod/src/v4/core/schemas.ts
@@ -4397,13 +4397,17 @@ export interface $ZodFunction<
   implement<F extends $InferInnerFunctionType<Args, Returns>>(
     func: F
   ): // allow for return type inference
-  (
+  ((
     ...args: Parameters<this["_output"]>
-  ) => ReturnType<F> extends ReturnType<this["_output"]> ? ReturnType<F> : ReturnType<this["_output"]>;
+  ) => ReturnType<F> extends ReturnType<this["_output"]> ? ReturnType<F> : ReturnType<this["_output"]>) & {
+    _zod: $ZodFunctionInternals<Args, Returns>;
+  };

   implementAsync<F extends $InferInnerFunctionTypeAsync<Args, Returns>>(
     func: F
-  ): F extends $InferOuterFunctionTypeAsync<Args, Returns> ? F : $InferOuterFunctionTypeAsync<Args, Returns>;
+  ): (F extends $InferOuterFunctionTypeAsync<Args, Returns> ? F : $InferOuterFunctionTypeAsync<Args, Returns>) & {
+    _zod: $ZodFunctionInternals<Args, Returns>;
+  };

   input<const Items extends util.TupleItems, const Rest extends $ZodFunctionOut = $ZodFunctionOut>(
     args: Items,
@@ -4431,7 +4435,7 @@ export const $ZodFunction: core.$constructor<$ZodFunction> = /*@__PURE__*/ core.
       if (typeof func !== "function") {
         throw new Error("implement() must be called with a function");
       }
-      return function (this: any, ...args: never[]) {
+      const implemented = function (this: any, ...args: never[]) {
         const parsedArgs = inst._def.input ? parse(inst._def.input, args) : args;
         const result = Reflect.apply(func, this, parsedArgs as never[]);
         if (inst._def.output) {
@@ -4439,20 +4443,30 @@ export const $ZodFunction: core.$constructor<$ZodFunction> = /*@__PURE__*/ core.
         }
